<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Rapa Nui</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Polynesian society of Easter Island, the most remote inhabited island in the Pacific, famous for its giant moai statues and the undeciphered rongorongo script.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Polynesian voyagers reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> around 1200, probably from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangareva">Mangareva</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas</a>. Island tradition names the founding chief as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hotu-matua">Hotu Matuʻa</a>, who landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anakena">Anakena Bay</a> and divided the land among his sons, ancestors of the island's clans. Over the next centuries the population grew to several thousand.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kin groups (mata) each held a wedge of coast and built stone platforms (ahu) carrying moai carved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rano-raraku">Rano Raraku</a> crater, nearly 900 in all. A paramount ariki of the Miru clan held sacred rank. Later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangata-manu">Tangata manu</a> or birdman cult at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orongo">Orongo</a> chose a yearly leader through a race to collect the first sooty tern egg. People farmed sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> in stone-mulched gardens and rock shelters on a largely treeless island.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-roggeveen">Jacob Roggeveen</a> arrived on Easter Day 1722. Peruvian slave raids in 1862 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> that followed cut the population to around 110 by 1877. In 1888 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> annexed the island through an agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/policarpo-toro">Policarpo Toro</a> and the ariki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atamu-tekena">Atamu Tekena</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> remains part of Chile.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1200 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:13.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hotu Matuʻa, Ngaʻara, Atamu Tekena</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Polynesian religion, Makemake cult</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rapa Nui</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Easter Island chiefdom, Rapa Nui kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1544&amp;focus=rapa-nui" aria-label="Rapa Nui on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1803 3172 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rapa Nui lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2123 3428L3195 3647"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3195 3647h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2123 3428h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2123 3428h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1544&amp;focus=rapa-nui">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

